{
	title: 'Tab commands run on the host: /new, /close, /resume',
	spec: "Tabs are host state, so the tab commands are host commands: src/host/commands/new.ts, close.ts and resume.ts, not clientOnly in the shared command list, with no src/client/commands files. They act on the session that ran them, so they work the same typed in the terminal or the web app and sent from another session (/send, the send tool). /new [dir] opens a tab right after the running session, in its cwd unless a directory is given. /close [tab] closes the running session's tab, or the named one (tab number or session id); a running turn stops as with tab-close today. /resume [id] reopens the most recently closed tab, or the named closed session, right after the running session. After /new and /resume the host sends a go event, so clients showing the running session switch to the opened tab; clients showing a closed tab move on as they do now. The keys Ctrl-T, Ctrl-W and Shift-Ctrl-T run these commands like any typed command (the web keeps its browser-reserved key exceptions). The tab-new, tab-close and tab-resume protocol messages stay for the tab bar and other tab UI; the commands reuse tabs.act and add no new tab logic. Client-only commands are only those acting on the client process or its screen: /quit, /suspend, /redraw. Tests: /close and /new sent from another session act on the named or running tab, not the sender's focused tab.",
	status: 'planned',
